        <p>
          The biggest surprise in the ranking is California. Despite
          its reputation for high taxes, its progressive brackets leave
          middle earners better off than Oregon, Hawaii, Maine and
          others on $100,000 — California ranks 44th, not last, with
          $73,957 of take-home, or $6,163.08 a month. New York is a
          similar story: it ranks 41st at $74,320 ($6,193.33 a month),
          but remember that local taxes are not counted — New York
          City's income tax would push its real take-home lower. Check
          the
          <a href="/en-us/states/california-paycheck-calculator/">California
          paycheck calculator</a>, the
          <a href="/en-us/states/new-york-paycheck-calculator/">New
          York paycheck calculator</a> or the
          <a href="/en-us/states/oregon-paycheck-calculator/">Oregon
          paycheck calculator</a> for the full breakdown in any of
          these states.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="how-calculated" class="article-section">
        <h2>How the Figures Are Calculated</h2>
        <p>
          Every figure in the ranking comes from the same setup, so
          the states are directly comparable. The study assumes a
          <strong>single filer</strong> earning
          <strong>$100,000 in wages</strong> with
          <strong>no pre-tax deductions</strong> — no 401(k), no
          health-insurance premiums taken out before tax. It applies
          the 2026 IRS federal brackets, the 2026 Social Security wage
          base of $184,500, and each state's 2026 income tax brackets.
          Because $100,000 is below the $184,500 wage base, Social
          Security applies to the full salary in this study.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three things are deliberately left out. State personal
          exemptions and credits are not modeled, local (city and
          county) income taxes are not modeled, and the figures are
          estimates — your real paycheck will differ if you have
          pre-tax deductions, a different filing status, or income
          other than wages. That is why the study's own page says the
          figures are estimates and points readers to the
          <a href="/en-us/states/">state paycheck calculators</a> to
          run their own numbers. The method is the same one behind our
          <a href="/en-us/blog/how-to-calculate-your-paycheck-2026/">paycheck
          guide</a>: start with gross pay, subtract each tax in turn,
          and what is left is take-home pay.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="your-state" class="article-section">
        <h2>What to Do With Your State's Number</h2>
        <p>
          A ranking is only useful if it changes a decision, so here
          is how to use it:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Find your state.</strong> The full 50-state
            ranking, with every state from Alaska to Oregon, is on our
            <a href="/en-us/study/take-home-pay-100k-by-state/">2026
            study page</a>. If your salary is not $100,000, run your
            own salary through your state's paycheck calculator
            instead — the ranking order can shift at other incomes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Compare the monthly figure, not just the annual
            one.</strong> A $681.33 monthly gap is the number that
            shows up in a budget; the annual figure is what it adds
            up to. Our
            <a href="/en-us/blog/how-much-rent-can-i-afford-2026/">rent
            affordability guide</a> and
            <a href="/en-us/blog/how-much-house-can-you-afford-2026/">house
            affordability guide</a> both start from monthly take-home
            pay, so use the monthly number there.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Remember what is not counted.</strong> Local taxes
            are excluded, so if you live in a city with its own income
            tax, your real take-home is lower than the ranking shows.
            Cost of living is not in the study at all — a state with
            no income tax can still cost more overall once housing
            and other prices are counted.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Use it for planning, not for a move.</strong>
            State tax is one line on a paystub, not a reason to
            relocate on its own. If you are weighing an offer in
            another state, compare the full package — salary, state
            tax, local tax and living costs — with the same
            calculator, and check what the extra take-home is worth
            against the move itself.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The ranking also settles a common argument: a high-tax
          reputation does not always mean the lowest take-home pay.
          California at 44th and New York at 41st both beat Oregon,
          Hawaii and Maine on $100,000 — and the nine states at the
          top are there for one reason only, that they charge $0 of
          state income tax. Run your state, your salary and your filing
          status through the calculator before drawing any conclusion
          about your own paycheck.
        </p>
      </section>

